<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A school cook mixes maize and beans in the ratio <m>{3} : {2}</m> by mass to make <m>{40}</m> kg of githeri mix. Maize costs Ksh <m>{50}</m> a kilogram and beans Ksh <m>{150}</m> a kilogram.</p><p>(a) What mass of beans is used? Give your answer exactly or correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p><fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/> kg  </p><p>(b) What does 1 kg of the mix cost, to the nearest shilling?</p><p>Ksh <fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/>  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) There are <m>{3} + {2} = {5}</m> parts, so one part is <m>{40} \div {5} = {8}</m> kg. Beans: <m>{2} \times {8} = {16}</m> kg (and maize <m>{24}</m> kg).</p><p>(b) The whole mix costs <m>{24} \times {50} + {16} \times {150} = {3600}</m> shillings for <m>{40}</m> kg, so 1 kg costs <m>{3600} \div {40} = {90}</m>, that is Ksh <m>{90}</m> to the nearest shilling.</p><p>The cost of a mixture leans towards the ingredient you use more of. More maize than beans is used, so the cost is nearer the maize price than the mean, Ksh <m>{100}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>16</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>90</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>